==English==

===Etymology===
{{confix|iso|morphism}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-isomorphism.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Similarity of form
#* '''1984''' Brigitte Asbach-Schnitker, "Introduction", ''Mercury or The Secret and Swift Messenger'', ISBN 9027232768.
#*: The postulated '''isomorphism''' between words and things constitutes the characterizing feature of all philosophically based universal languages.
:# {{context|biology|lang=en}} the [[similarity]] in [[form]] of [[organism]]s of different [[ancestry]]
:# {{context|chemistry|lang=en}} the similarity in the [[crystal]] structures of similar [[chemical]] [[compound]]s
:#* '''1874''' C. Rammelsberg, [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=jOUEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA197#v=onepage&q&f=true "Crystallographic and chemical relations of the natural sulphides, arsenides, and sulpharsenides"], ''The Chemical News and Journal of Physical Science'', page 197.
:#*: The '''isomorphism''' of compounds does not prove the '''isomorphism''' of their respective constituents.
:# {{context|sociology|lang=en}} the similarity in the structure or [[processes]] of different [[organization]]s
: &nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;A one-to-one correspondence
:# {{context|group algebra|lang=en}} A [[bijection]] ''f'' such that both ''f'' and its [[inverse function|inverse]] ''f''<sup>&nbsp;&minus;1</sup> are [[homomorphism]]s, that is, structure-preserving mappings.
:# {{context|computer science|lang=en}} a [[one-to-one]] [[correspondence]] between all the [[element]]s of two [[set]]s, e.g. the [[instance]]s of two [[class]]es, or the [[record]]s in two [[dataset]]s
:# {{context|category theory|lang=en}} A [[morphism]] which has an inverse; the composition of the morphism and its inverse yields either one of two identity morphisms (depending on the order of composition).

